  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will be in place across the area ahead of the \nDesert Southwest closed upper low will swing eastward toward the \nSouthern and Central Plains Wednesday. At the same time, the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo and it's associated tropical moisture, \nwill be pulled northeast and become absorbed into the upper \ncirculation of the upper low by Wednesday. Operational models as \nwell as the Ensemble members of the ECMWF and GFS are starting to \nfocus the potential for heavy rainfall farther to the north \nimpacting western and north central Nebraska. \n\nOn Tuesday, the upper low should be located across Arizona. Mid and \nupper level sub-tropical moisture, with the remnants Hurricane Polo, \nwill be streaming northeastward across New Mexico into Colorado, \nKansas and Nebraska ahead of the low. At the surface, low pressure \nshould deepening across eastern Colorado, with an inverted surface \ntrough extending northeastward across southwest into north central \nNebraska. The low-levels will also be very moist, with the NAM \nprojecting lower to mid 60s dew points in place. This model is the \nfarther west and focuses some very heavy rainfall amounts near the \ninverted trough. Other models are not as aggressive, so this will \nhave to be monitored. \n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, the surface low associated with former \nHurricane Polo will track northward across New Mexico and into \nsoutheast Colorado by midday Wednesday. This has been supported by \nlast several runs of the ECMWF and the ENS. Heavy rainfall will be \nassociated with this feature, with widespread amounts of 2\"+ across \nmuch of eastern Colorado into western Kansas. The heavier rainfall \nhas shifted a bit northward into southern Nebraska compared to 24 \nhrs ago. The latest 27/00Z operational run of the ECMWF is \nprojecting a band of 1-3\" of rainfall roughly along I-80 and points \nsouth to along the Nebraska/Kansas border. The GFS is now similar, \nwith even heavier amounts. The key will be the northern stream. \nTrends are for a the northern stream to remain farther north across \nMontana, through North Dakota and into northern Minnesota. This \nallows for the tropical rainfall from Polo to shift into southern \nNebraska. There will be a rather sharp cut-off to the precipitation \non the northern side, so future forecasts will have to be monitored \nfor a better idea of where this cut-off will be. Bottom line is \nthere will be potential for excessive rainfall amounts across a \nlarge portions of the region. Some flooding could materialize so \nfuture model runs and trends will be monitored carefully.  \n\nBy Thursday, it appears that the northern stream flow will begin to \nsag southward and this will focus the heavier rainfall south and \neast of the area. In addition, a cooler Canadian airmass will sink \nsouthward into the region. Mainly dry weather is expected Friday \ninto Saturday.",
